<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Galicia</h1><p class="st-meta">1084 CE – 1199 CE (approx.) · capital Halych</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A rich principality on the upper Dniester, built around the salt trade and the town of Halych, whose boyars were famously powerful.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the 1080s three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurikid</a> brothers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik">Rurik</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volodar-rostyslavych">Volodar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasylko-rostyslavych">Vasylko Rostislavich</a>, took control of the towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/przemysl">Peremyshl</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zvenyhorod-lviv-oblast">Zvenyhorod</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terebovlia">Terebovl</a> in the far south-west of Rus'. Their heir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volodymyrko-volodarovych">Volodymyrko</a> united these lands and in 1141 made Halych on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a> his capital, creating the Principality of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-galicia-and-lodomeria">Galicia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-galicia-and-lodomeria">Galicia</a> prospered from salt mines, which supplied much of Rus', and from trade along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Its landholding boyars were unusually strong and repeatedly deposed or defied their princes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-osmomysl">Yaroslav Osmomysl</a>, who ruled from 1153 to 1187, was praised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-tale-of-igors-campaign">Lay of Igor's Campaign</a> as a mighty ruler.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volodymyrko-volodarovych">Volodymyrko</a> died out with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-yaroslavich-of-galicia">Vladimir Yaroslavich</a> in 1199. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-the-great">Roman Mstislavich</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-volhynia">Principality of Volhynia</a> then took Halych and joined the two lands into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-galicia-volhynia">Kingdom of Galicia-Volhynia</a>, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> long contested <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-galicia-and-lodomeria">Galicia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-galicia.jpg" alt="Principality of Galicia" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Roman Frankiv · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Halych_Principality.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1084 CE – 1199 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Halych</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Volodymyrko, Yaroslav Osmomysl, Vladimir Yaroslavich</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old East Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Ukraine, Poland</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Principality of Halych, Halych</dd></dl></aside></div>
